speechtext:
I have the honour to inform the House that during the recess I received communications from several members, notifying me that the following vacancies had occurred in the representation, viz:
Of William Folger Nickle, Esq:. Member for the Electoral District of Kingston, by resignation;
Of Simon Fraser Tolmie, Esq., Member for the Electoral District of Victoria City, consequent upon the acceptance of an office of emolument under the Crown, to wit: the office of Minister of Agriculture;
Of John Gillaniers Turriff, Esq., Member for the Electoral District of Assiniboia, consequent upon being summoned to the Senate;
Of the Right Honourable Sir Wilfrid Laurier, G.C.M.G., Member for the Electoral District of Quebec, East, by decease;
Of Joseph Read, Esq., Member for the Electoral District of Prince, (P.E.I.), by decease;
Of Hon. Frank Broadstreet Carvell, Member for the Electoral District of Victoria and Carleton, consequent upon the acceptance of an office of emolument under the Crown.
I accordingly issued my several warrants to the Clerk of the Crown in Chancery to make out new writs of election for the said electoral districts respectively.

1.	Has the attention of the Government been directed to a resolution which has recently been passed by the Municipal Council of the City of Montreal, and which in effect is as follows:
"Whereas the war has ended, and enlistments of men in Canada have stopped;
"Whereas certain countries, more particularly that of France, have shown themselves rather lenient towards those who frustrated the Act relating to military service;
'Whereas Canada, in its status of a (British colony, has nobly done its duty towards the Mother Country ;
'Whereas His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales will do us the honour of visiting our city for a few days in the month of October;
'iBe it resolved:
26 i
"That His Royal Highness be respectfully approached upon the occasion of his visit to the metropolis of Canada, and entreated with a view to obtaining an amnesty in favour of those who, by failing to enlist, did not conform to the provisions of the Act respecting military service; also that the penalty which the tribunals imposed as to fines, be revoked and that the said fines be remitted."
2.	If so, will the said resolution receive the approbation of the Government?

It appears that it was the unanimous conclusion of that very important commission. If that is the case in Canada, and T say it is, the motion of the hon, member can be understood by the Government to mean that the most careful consideration should be. given to circumstances of that character, and I should regard it as a very important part of the Government's functions, through a committee or of their own motion, or by whatever machinery they may devise, to find out for themselves if the statements made by the hon. member, and which were made by myself on my responsibility in the House when speaking upon the Budget not very long ago, are correct, and, if they are, to ascertain what the proper remedy is. When the National Policy was adopted in Canada, a policy which to some extent was a trade restriction and trade preference, it was contended by its opponents that it would simply create a condition such as we have to-day, a condition of combines, trusts, and so forth. But as Sir John A. Macdonald and Sir Charles Tupper said on that occasion, there was no danger of anything of that kind, since honest local competition between different trades or between different men in the same trade would keep healthy and pure the commercial atmosphere of Canada and we would have no combines. There are in the United States trusts in every line of business-the beef trust, the ice trust, the flour trust, the sugar trust-trusts in connection with every necessary of life, and we have the same trusts in Canada, not on such a huge scale, because we have not the population or the wealth. But in miniature every one of those trusts exists in Canada. They may not, perhaps, be known as trusts;
they may not have that particular appellation, but they embody the same principle. The combination exists, whether we can discover it or not, and the poor man who has to buy is in the last analysis the sufferer.
I submit, therefore, that the speech of the hon. member for Peterborough (Mr. Burnham) to-day is fraught with information of a most valuable character, and I think that he has imparted to the Government and to this House a thought that is well worthy of our careful consideration. I do not know that I am in a position to tell the Government where they should begin in regard to these things, but the trouble exists. There is an arrangement, either a written arrangement or a gentleman's agreement, in connection with every* line of production in this country, by which prices are not governed by competition and trade but production is regulated so that it will not exceed the demand, and sales may be kept within the limits of the quantities which it is proposed shall be sold, and at prices agreed upon. These are matters which the Government can ascertain. It is not for me to .say how; but as the leader of the Government has said, the Government is responsible to the people, and I have no desire to take any illegitimate or direct means. of displacing or surplanting them for the moment. I want to do that in the proper legitimate way when the proper time comes. But while the Government is in power it is their duty to inquire into these matters and find an adequate solution for them. The hon. member for Peterborough requires the Government to deal with national matters in a scientific way. Well, I am afraid he might as well .ask that the propagation of Confucianism in Canada should he undertaken by the Methodist church as to ask this Government to conduct matters in .a scientific way.

I want to read a couple of sentences from page 4X1 of Unrevised Hansard and to make denial with regard to them. The hon. member said:
I had an opportunity of visiting the front in 1916 ; the member for Russell makes reference to that occasion. I am sorry that he did not have the opportunity of visiting the front;
And on that he bases a cheap sneer at me with reference to recruiting. I took every possible part in recruiting that those who claimed a monopoly of the war would allow me to take. The hon. gentleman omitted to tell you that in order to make that trip to the front, he received about $3,750 from a group of gentlemen in Toronto.

Oh, no, there have to be two applications. You have only to give one notice but you have to make two applications. You have got to do a lot of negotiating with the Department of Marine and Fisheries before you can get a corner or a lot to set up a structure in a harbour.
Mr. SIFT'ON: The Minister of Public Works does not sign the 'authority until that has been done.
Mr. BUREAU": I am speaking, of a case where there has been approval from- the Minister of Public Works without getting a lease from the Minister of Marine. I know of one caise anyway where that occurred, through error or otherwise. Would this clause legalize such a proceeding in the event of any parties wanting to take advantage of the fact that all the conditions of the statute had not. been complied [DOT] with;
